<.shell current={:drag_list}>
  <h1>Drag list</h1>
  <p class="lead">Rows people put in order: drag a row, or move it with the keyboard.</p>

  <div class="demo max-w-xl">
    <.drag_list
      :let={{_key, title}}
      id="drag-list-demo"
      rows={[
        {"1", "People helped"},
        {"2", "Requesting agency"},
        {"3", "Team notes"},
        {"4", "Debrief held"}
      ]}
      key={&elem(&1, 0)}
      label={&elem(&1, 1)}
      event="reorder"
      moves
    >
      {title}
    </.drag_list>
  </div>

  <h3>Inside a form</h3>
  <p>Each row holds its own inputs and buttons, such as a field's choices.</p>
  <div class="demo max-w-xl">
    <.drag_list
      :let={{key, label}}
      id="drag-list-demo-choices"
      rows={[{"1", "Police"}, {"2", "Ambulance"}, {"3", "Fire"}]}
      key={&elem(&1, 0)}
      label={&elem(&1, 1)}
      event="reorder"
    >
      <input
        type="text"
        id={"drag-list-demo-choice-#{key}"}
        value={label}
        aria-label={"Choice #{key}"}
        class="grow"
      />
      <.button type="button" size={:sm}>Archive</.button>
    </.drag_list>
  </div>

  <h2>When to use</h2>
  <p>
    A short list whose order people choose and others see, such as a team's fields on Change order or a field's choices. Changing the order is the point of the list, or one part of a form.
  </p>

  <h2>When not to use</h2>
  <p>
    A list people read in an order they don't set, such as members by name. Use a <a href={
      page_path(:table)
    }>table</a>, sorted by a column. A long list, past about 20 rows, where a number or a date sets the order better than dragging.
  </p>

  <h2>How it works</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Drag.</strong>
      The grip handle drags its row, with a mouse, a finger, or a pen. On a touch screen the handle takes the drag rather than scrolling the page, and its target is 44px. Near the top or bottom of the window the page scrolls.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Keyboard.</strong>
      The handle is a button, Move and the row's name. With it focused, the up and down arrows move the row one place, and focus stays on it.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Move up and Move down.</strong>
      Where ordering is the whole page, each row has both buttons too. The first row's Move up and the last row's Move down grey out.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Screen readers</strong> hear where the row went: "Team notes, 2 of 4".
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Nothing is saved as rows move. The page's own button saves the order, such as Save order, or Save field for choices.
  </p>

  <h2>In the app</h2>
  <p>
    <code>&lt;.drag_list id="…" rows=&#123;@fields&#125; key=&#123;&amp;to_string(&amp;1.id)&#125; label=&#123;&amp; &amp;1.title&#125; event="reorder" moves :let=&#123;field&#125;&gt;</code>
    draws a row for each, with the row's content from its block. <code>key</code>
    gives a row's key, a string, and <code>label</code>
    its name for the handle. The <code>DragList</code>
    hook moves the row at once and sends <code>event</code>
    with <code>%&#123;"keys" =&gt; keys&#125;</code>, every key in the new order. The LiveView puts its list in that order with
    <code>Web.Components.DragList.reorder/3</code>
    and renders it. Leave out <code>moves</code>
    for the handle alone.
  </p>
</.shell>
